  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAnother hot and humid afternoon is underway, with heat indices now\nin the 105-110F range across much of the northern 2/3rd of the\narea. Areas further south got a later start due to morning low\nclouds, but they are into the 100-105F range and climbing. A weak\ncold front is approaching from the north. Ahead of it,\nmesoanalysis indicates steepening low level lapse rates within a \nmoist airmass. Scattered storms are beginning to develop in this \nregime and will continue to do so through later this afternoon. \nThe aforementioned instability combined with increasing upper flow\nand strong DCAPE suggests a greater potential for damaging wind \ngusts with storms that develop today. We may also some of these \nstorms organize into multicell clusters that might contribute to \nstrong wind gusts along southward moving outflows. Don't be\nsurprised to see some severe thunderstorm warnings later this\nafternoon. Coverage and intensity are expected to decrease \nquickly after sunset.\n\nFor Labor Day, the weak front will bring a brief spell of\nrelatively drier air over parts of north MS and AR, potentially \nencroaching closer to the I-20 corridor during the day. While this\ncould help to limit heat indices the farther north one goes in our\narea, it is still expected to be quite hot in these areas \nnevertheless. And further south, the humidity will remain; thus, \nanother heat advisory has been posted for Monday across much of\nthe area. The front will also help to limit rain chances across \nmost of the area. However, south of the front there could be a \nrepeat performance of today across central LA into south MS. At \nthis point, it's unclear if storms would initiate in the Natchez- \nHattiesburg corridor or just south of our area. Nevertheless, \nthough there is currently no formal severe threat, we can't rule\nout damaging wind potential on Labor Day afternoon. \n\nTuesday through the end of the week: The weak front will wash \nout, with low level moisture recovering again areawide Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. Conditions will continue to be seasonably warm \neach day, and it's possible heat stress potential will be high \nenough for additional heat advisories in some areas, though \nforecast heat indices are looking more marginal than what has been\nexperienced in recent days. Mainly diurnal convection will remain\npossible, initially focused midweek mainly across south MS and \nLA, but expanding northward through the end of the week. /DL/",
